A causal LTI system is governed by \[y(t)+\frac{1}{4}\frac{dy(t)}{dt}=2x(t).\] Taking the Laplace transform under zero initial conditions, \[Y(s)+\frac{s}{4}Y(s)=2X(s).\] Hence, the transfer function is \[H(s)=\frac{Y(s)}{X(s)}
=\frac{2}{1+\frac{s}{4}}
=\frac{8}{s+4}.\] Taking the inverse Laplace transform, \[h(t)=8e^{-4t}u(t).\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }8e^{-4t}u(t)}\]

The impulse response is \[h(t)=2\left(\frac{\sin(10\pi t)}{\pi t}\right)\cos(40\pi t).\] Using the Fourier transform pair, \[2\frac{\sin(10\pi t)}{\pi t}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\begin{cases}
2, &amp; |\omega|&lt;10\pi,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\] Multiplication by \(\cos(40\pi t)\) shifts the spectrum to \[30\pi&lt;|\omega|&lt;50\pi.\] The input signal contains frequency components at \[10\pi,\;15\pi,\;42\pi,\;45\pi.\] Only the components at \(42\pi\) and \(45\pi\) lie within the passband. Hence, \[y(t)=7\sin(42\pi t)+4\cos(45\pi t).\] \[\boxed{\text{(c) }7\sin(42\pi t)+4\cos(45\pi t)}\]

Since the DTFS coefficients are periodic with period \(N\), \[a_k=a_{k+N}.\] Given, \[N=3,\qquad a_{-3}=2,\qquad a_4=1.\] Hence, \[a_{-3}=a_0=2,\qquad
a_4=a_1=1,\qquad
a_2=0.\] Therefore, \[x[n]
=\sum_{k=0}^{2}a_ke^{j\frac{2\pi}{3}kn}
=2+e^{j\frac{2\pi}{3}n}.\] Rewrite the constant term as \[2=1+1.\] Thus, \[x[n]
=1+\left(1+e^{j\frac{2\pi}{3}n}\right).\] Using \[1=e^{j\frac{\pi}{3}n}e^{-j\frac{\pi}{3}n},\] we get \[1+e^{j\frac{2\pi}{3}n}
=e^{j\frac{\pi}{3}n}
\left(e^{-j\frac{\pi}{3}n}+e^{j\frac{\pi}{3}n}\right).\] Applying \[e^{j\theta}+e^{-j\theta}=2\cos\theta,\] yields \[x[n]
=
1+
2e^{j\frac{\pi}{3}n}
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}n\right).\] Since \[\frac{\pi}{3}=\frac{2\pi}{6},\] the signal becomes \[x[n]
=
1+
2e^{j\left(\frac{2\pi}{6}n\right)}
\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{6}n\right).\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }1+2e^{j\left(\frac{2\pi}{6}n\right)}
\cos\left(\frac{2\pi}{6}n\right)}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>DTFS coefficients satisfy \(a_k=a_{k+N}\).</li><li>Convert all given indices to the range \(0\le k\le N-1\).</li></ul></div>

For a sinusoidal input, the steady-state output amplitude is obtained by multiplying the input amplitude by the magnitude of the frequency response. The transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{100}{s^2+0.1s+100}.\] For the DC component, \(\omega=0\): \[a=1\times |G(0)|
=1\times\frac{100}{100}
=1.\] For the sinusoidal component, \(\omega=10\,\text{rad/s}\): \[G(j10)=\frac{100}{(j10)^2+0.1(j10)+100}\] \[=\frac{100}{-100+j1+100}
=\frac{100}{j}
=-j100.\] Hence, \[|G(j10)|=100.\] The input sinusoid has amplitude \(0.1\), so the output amplitude is \[b=0.1\times |G(j10)|
=0.1\times100
=10.\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{(A) }a=1,\ b=10}\]

