By Ampere's law, \[\nabla\times\vec{H}=\vec{J}\] Given, \[\vec{H}
=
x^2\,\hat{i}
+
x^2y^2\,\hat{j}
+
x^2y^2z^2\,\hat{k}\] Evaluating the curl, \[\vec{J}
=
\begin{vmatrix}
\hat{i} &amp; \hat{j} &amp; \hat{k}\\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} &amp;
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial y} &amp;
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial z}\\
x^2 &amp; x^2y^2 &amp; x^2y^2z^2
\end{vmatrix}\] \[\vec{J}
=
\hat{i}
\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2y^2z^2)
-
\frac{\partial}{\partial z}(x^2y^2)
\right)
-\hat{j}
\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2y^2z^2)
-
\frac{\partial}{\partial z}(x^2)
\right)
+\hat{k}
\left(
\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2y^2)
-
\frac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2)
\right)\] \[\vec{J}
=
\hat{i}(2x^2yz^2-0)
-\hat{j}(2xy^2z^2-0)
+\hat{k}(2xy^2-0)\] \[\vec{J}
=
2x^2yz^2\,\hat{i}
-
2xy^2z^2\,\hat{j}
+
2xy^2\,\hat{k}\] At \[x=1\,\text{m},
\qquad
y=2\,\text{m},
\qquad
z=1\,\text{m}\] \[\vec{J}
=
4\hat{i}
-
8\hat{j}
+
8\hat{k}\] Hence, \[|\vec{J}|
=
\sqrt{4^2+(-8)^2+8^2}\] \[|\vec{J}|
=
\sqrt{144}
=
12\,\text{A/m}^2\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{B}\]

Given: \[V = 280\,\text{V}, \quad R_{a} = 1\,\Omega, \quad R_{ext} = 10\,\Omega\] \[I_{a1} = 30\,\text{A}, \quad N_{1} = 1000\,\text{rpm}\] For a separately excited DC motor with constant flux, \[T \propto \phi I_{a}\] Since flux is constant, \[T \propto I_{a}\] Also, given \(T \propto N\), \[\frac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} = \frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}}\] Hence, \[\frac{I_{a2}}{I_{a1}} = \frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}}\] Substituting values, \[I_{a2} = \frac{30}{1000}N_{2}\] \[I_{a2} = 0.03N_{2}\] For constant flux, \[N \propto E_{b}\] Therefore, \[\frac{N_{2}}{N_{1}} = \frac{E_{b2}}{E_{b1}}\] Using back emf equation, \[\frac{N_{2}}{1000}
=
\frac{280 - I_{a2}(R_{a}+R_{ext})}{280 - I_{a1}R_{a}}\] Substituting values, \[\frac{N_{2}}{1000}
=
\frac{280 - 0.03N_{2}(1+10)}{280 - 30(1)}\] \[\frac{N_{2}}{1000}
=
\frac{280 - 0.33N_{2}}{250}\] \[250N_{2} = 280000 - 330N_{2}\] \[580N_{2} = 280000\] \[N_{2} = 482.76\,\text{rpm}\] \[\boxed{483}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For separately excited DC motors, flux remains constant.</li><li>Torque is directly proportional to armature current.</li><li>Speed is proportional to back emf when flux is constant.</li></ul></div>

Given, \[f=40\,\text{Hz}, \qquad f_{r}=1\,\text{Hz}, \qquad P=8\] Slip is \[s=\frac{f_{r}}{f}=\frac{1}{40}=0.025\] Synchronous speed is \[N_{s}=\frac{120f}{P}
=\frac{120\times 40}{8}
=600\,\text{rpm}\] Rotor speed is \[N=N_{s}(1-s)\] \[N=600(1-0.025)=585\,\text{rpm}\] \[\boxed{585}\]

Given, \[V_L=400\,\text{V}\] \[X_s=1\,\Omega/\text{phase}\] \[P_{out}=10\,\text{kW}\] \[\eta=0.8,\qquad P_{loss}=2\,\text{kW}\] Neglect armature resistance. \[P_{in}=P_{out}+P_{loss}=10+2=12\,\text{kW}\] \[I_a=\frac{P_{in}}{\sqrt{3}V_L\cos\phi}
=
\frac{12000}{\sqrt{3}\times 400\times 0.8}
=
21.65\,\text{A}\] \[V=\frac{400}{\sqrt{3}}\] For a synchronous motor, \[E_b=
\sqrt{
\left(V\cos\phi\right)^2+
\left(V\sin\phi+I_aX_s\right)^2
}\] \[E_b=
\sqrt{
\left(\frac{400}{\sqrt{3}}\times 0.8\right)^2
+
\left(\frac{400}{\sqrt{3}}\times 0.6+21.65\times 1\right)^2
}\] \[E_b=
\sqrt{
(184.75)^2+(138.56+21.65)^2
}\] \[E_b=244.55\,\text{V per phase}\] \[\boxed{245}\]

Given: \[V_{LL}=100\sqrt{3}\,\text{V}\] \[Z_1=(18+j24)\,\Omega\] \[Z_2=(6+j8)\,\Omega\] The inner star-connected load is first converted into an equivalent delta load. For balanced star-to-delta conversion, \[Z_{\Delta}=3Z_2\] Hence, the equivalent per-phase impedance becomes \[Z_{ph}=Z_1 \parallel 3Z_2\] \[Z_{ph}
=
\frac{Z_1(3Z_2)}{Z_1+3Z_2}\] \[Z_{ph}
=
\frac{(18+j24)(18+j24)}
{(18+j24)+(18+j24)}\] \[Z_{ph}
=
15\angle 53.1^\circ\,\Omega\] After conversion, the network becomes a balanced delta load. <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> For a delta connection, \[V_{ph}=V_{LL}\] \[I_{ph}=\frac{V_{ph}}{Z_{ph}}\] \[I_{ph}
=
\frac{100\sqrt{3}}
{15\angle53.1^\circ}\] \[|I_{ph}|=11.547\,\text{A}\] Line current is \[I_L=\sqrt{3}\,I_{ph}\] \[I_L=\sqrt{3}\times11.547\] \[I_L=20\,\text{A}\] Therefore, the ammeter reading is \[\boxed{20}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Balanced star impedance converts to delta impedance as \(Z_{\Delta}=3Z_Y\).</li><li>For balanced delta loads, \(V_{ph}=V_{LL}\).</li><li>Line current in a delta connection is \(\sqrt{3}\) times the phase current.</li></ul></div>

For a composite conductor having four equal strands, the self-GMD (GMR) is obtained by taking the geometric mean of all distances from one strand to every strand, including the self-distance. <img src="img/4c6fdd33da9a5a23.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,269px)"> From the geometry, \[D_{11}=r'=0.7788\,r\] \[D_{12}=2r\] \[D_{13}=2\sqrt{2}\,r\] \[D_{14}=2r\] Hence, \[\text{GMR}
=
\sqrt[4]{D_{11}D_{12}D_{13}D_{14}}\] \[\text{GMR}
=
\sqrt[4]{(0.7788\,r)(2r)(2\sqrt{2}\,r)(2r)}\] \[\text{GMR}
=
1.723\,r\] \[\boxed{\text{(D)} \enspace 1.723\,r}\]

Given that only \(5\%\) of the supplied power reaches the output terminal, \[P_{\text{out}} = 0.05\,P_{\text{in}}\] Power loss in decibels is \[\text{Loss (dB)}
=
10\log_{10}\left(\frac{P_{\text{in}}}{P_{\text{out}}}\right)\] Substituting, \[\text{Loss (dB)}
=
10\log_{10}\left(\frac{P_{\text{in}}}{0.05\,P_{\text{in}}}\right)\] \[\text{Loss (dB)}
=
10\log_{10}(20)\] \[\text{Loss (dB)}
=
13.01\,\text{dB}\] Rounding off to the nearest integer, \[\boxed{13}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use \(20\log_{10}\) since the problem involves power ratio, not voltage ratio.</li></ul></div>

Given complex power: \[S=(-4000-j3000)\,\text{VA}\] Hence, \[P_o=4000\,\text{W}, \qquad
|S|=\sqrt{4000^2+3000^2}=5000\,\text{VA}\] Power factor: \[\text{PF}=\frac{P_o}{|S|}=\frac{4000}{5000}=0.8\] Using three-phase power relation, \[P_o=\sqrt{3}\,V_{L1}I_{0r}(\text{PF})\] Substituting \(I_{0r}=10\,\text{A}\): \[4000=\sqrt{3}\,V_{L1}\times10\times0.8\] \[V_{L1(\text{rms})}
=
\frac{4000}{8\sqrt{3}}
=
\frac{500}{\sqrt{3}}
\approx 288.675\,\text{V}\] For SPWM, \[V_{L1(\text{rms})}
=
M_A\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}V_s\right)\] Given \(V_s=1000\,\text{V}\): \[288.675
=
M_A\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}\times1000\right)\] \[M_A
=
\frac{288.675}{612.372}
\approx 0.47\] \[\boxed{0.47}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a three-phase system: \(P=\sqrt{3}V_L I_L \cos\phi\).</li><li>For linear SPWM: \(V_{L1(\text{rms})}=M_A\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2\sqrt{2}}V_s\right)\).</li></ul></div>
